# Untitled

## Step-Nucleation In Situ Self-Repair to Prepare Rollable Large-Area Ultrathin MOF Membranes
Ultrathin membranes with ultrahigh permeance and good gas selectivity have the potential to greatly decrease separation process costs, but it requires the practical preparation of large area membranes for implementation. Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are very attractive for membrane gas separation applications. However, to date, the largest MOF membrane area reported in the literature is only about \(100\;\mathsf{c m}^{2}\). In the present study, a new step-nucleation in situ self-repair strategy is proposed that enables the preparation of large-area \((2400\;\mathsf{c m}^{2})\) ultrathin and rollable MOF membranes deposited on an inexpensive flexible polymer membrane support layer for the first time, combining a polyvinyl alcohol (PVA)–metal-ion layer and a pure metal-ion layer. The main role of the pure metal-ion layer is to act as the main nucleation sites for MOF membrane growth, while the PVA–metal-ion layer acts as a slow-release metal-ion source, which supplements MOF crystal nucleation to repair any defects occurring. Membrane modules are necessary components for membrane applications, and spiral-wound modules are among the most common module formats that are widely applied in gas separation. A \(4800\;\mathsf{c m}^{2}\) spiral-wound membrane module was successfully prepared, demonstrating the practical implementation of large-area MOF membranes.
## 1. Introduction
Gas separation is one of the most important separation processes.[1–4] Over the last decade, much attention has been paid to ultrathin gas separation membranes with ultrahigh permeance and good selectivity, which can greatly decrease the process costs.[5–7] Compared with the conventional separation technologies having higher energy consumption and cost, membrane separation technology is being increasingly adopted in the fields of energy, chemical production, and environment, for several advantages including lower energy consumption and simpler continuous operation, especially gas separations such as alkanes/olefins and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\).[8,9] Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) present many advantages, such as adjustable structure, precise pore size, chemical function control, and large specific surface area, and have become one of the preferred materials for membrane separation technology.[10,11] Hollow fiber membrane substrates have advantages such as large
specific surface area, high flexibility, high loading density, excellent pressure resistance, and easy large-scale production,[12,13] so they are commonly used in industrial separation processes.[14,15] However, it is difficult to achieve difficult to grow ultrathin MOF selective-layer membranes onto hollow fiber polymeric and inorganic substrate, due to practical complexities.[13] Flat-sheet polymer membrane substrates have advantages such as more open access, flexibility, and processability, which is conducive to the preparation of large-area MOF membranes, and is convenient to apply in industrial applications.[16] In general, polysulfone (PSf) membrane substrate has good chemical stability, heat resistance, cheap and high mechanical strength, and is one of the most widely used substrates for reverse osmosis membranes and as ultrafiltration membranes.[17–20] Thus, PSf substrate plays an important role in the preparation of large-area MOF membranes. Many MOFs, such as ZIF-8, ZIF-67, ZIF-90, and CuBTC appear to be highly attractive for membrane gas separation applications.[21–23] In situ growth is one of the most common methods for the preparation of MOF membranes, and two general approaches are commonly reported. One is to fix the metal ions on an interface layer, which is then used to nucleate MOF membrane growth. However, using this approach, it
is difficult to avoid intergranular defects of MOF nuclei on the interface layer. The other method is to immobilize metal ions in a polymeric membrane support for MOF membrane growth. In our previous work, we prepared ultrathin low-crystallinity MOF membranes on polymeric membrane support layers that showed high \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8}\) separation performance.[22] In this case, the release of metal ions from the polymer interface layer is relatively slow, while the growth rate of MOF is relatively fast, making it difficult to avoid the emergence of defects over large-area membranes. In many gas separation processes such as alkanes/olefins and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) separations, the gas feed volume is large, resulting in a large membrane area requirement, so the preparation of large-area MOF membrane is one of the prerequisites for its industrial applications. According to the literature, there are several methods to prepare large-area MOF membranes, such as vapor deposition, semi-solid processing, and interfacial processing. Vapor deposition can deposit a uniform MOF layer on an uneven substrate, and the substrate does not need to be pretreated during the crystallization process.[24,25] There is good compatibility between the MOF layer and the substrate,[20] which is conducive to large-area preparation of the MOF membranes.[26] Semi-solid processing has the advantages of ultrafast, high yield, and reproducible preparation, and is expected to achieve continuous large-scale preparation of MOF membranes.[27] However, although the above two methods can be used effectively to prepare large-area MOF membranes, the MOF gas-selective layer tends to be too thick, which considerably reduces gas permeance. Interfacial processes are conducive to the preparation of large-area MOF membranes because of the advantages of good dispersion, interfacial compatibility, and fast reaction rate of MOF in the membrane.[28] Although the MOF membrane prepared by this method is very thin, defects readily occur when applied to a larger area, thus reducing the selectivity of the large-area MOF membrane. Therefore, existing methods of preparing large area MOF membrane are in need of improvement. To date, most studies focus on regulating thickness and morphology, while very few address the preparation of large-area membranes, with the maximum area reported being only about \(100\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\).[27,29] Furthermore, large-area ultrathin low-crystallinity MOF membranes are extremely challenging to prepare. Membrane modules are necessary components for the practical implementation of separation applications, and spiral-wound modules are among the most common module formats[30–34] that are widely applied in gas separation. The membranes employed in spiral-wound modules are characteristically rollable, but MOF membranes have characteristically poor rolling ability due to the limitations of current preparation methods. Both of these limitations impede the fabrication of MOF membrane modules and largely restrain their practical implementation.[33] Consequently, developing a simple and practical method that can realize large-area ultrathin and rollable MOF membranes is of great importance.[29] In the present study, we propose a new step-nucleation in situ self-repair strategy that enables the preparation of large-area \((2400~\mathrm{cm}^{2})\) ultrathin and rollable MOF membranes deposited on an inexpensive flexible polymer membrane support layer for the first time, combining a polyvinyl alcohol (PVA)–metal-ion layer and a pure metal-ion layer. The main role of the pure metal-ion layer is to act as the main nucleation sites for MOF membrane growth, while the PVA–metal-ion layer acts as a slow-release metal-ion source,
which supplements MOF crystal nucleation to repair any defects occurring. The \(4800\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) spiral-wound membrane modules also have been successfully prepared, demonstrating the practical implementation of large-area MOF membranes.
## 2. Results and Discussion
A PSf ultrafiltration membrane (average pore size \(20{-}30\,\mathrm{nm}\) ) was used as the support substrate, as shown in Figure S2 in the Supporting Information. The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images show large pores on the membrane surface (Figure S2, Supporting Information), and the membrane displays no appreciable gas selectivity, as expected (Figure S3, Supporting Information). An ultrathin polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) layer with a dry thickness of \(65\,\mathrm{nm}\) was coated to the PSf support layer, hereafter denoted as mPSf, to obtain a uniform and defect-free surface for MOF membrane growth, as shown in Figure S4 in the Supporting Information. The resulting mPSf support membrane has a high pure-gas permeance (25 014, 22 632, and 20 387 GPU for \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) , \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) , and \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}\) , respectively) and no appreciable selectivity for \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) , \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}\) , and \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8}\) mixtures (Figure S5, Supporting Information).
Figure 1a shows the formation process of the MOF membranes. The PVA–metal-ion and pure metal-ion layers were prepared on the mPSf support membrane, allowing the formation of a large-area ultrathin and defect-free MOF membrane layer. Details of the preparation are provided in the Supporting Information and Figures S6 and S7 in the Supporting Information. The pure metal-ion layer serves as the metal source of the MOF layer. Density functional theory calculations confirm the formation process of the membranes. As shown in Figures S8–S10 in the Supporting Information, ligand linker molecules first associate with the pure metal-ion layer to form a coordination complex, after which an ultrathin MOF layer is formed. However, many metal ions in the pure metal-ion layer dissolve into solution, and the occurrence of defects is difficult to avoid. In contrast with other reported MOF formation methods, the PVA–metal-ion layer acts as an intermediate layer to enable a step-nucleation in situ self-repair process, which is employed for the first time to obtain large-area defect-free MOF membranes. Owing to the swelling effect of solvent (water or ethanol) present during the MOF formation process, metal ions in the PVA–metal-ion layer are slowly and continuously released and step-diffuse into the defect region, where they serve as a metal-ion source to facilitate MOF growth and self-repair any defects. Simultaneously, ligand molecules are attracted to the PVA–metal-ion layer to form a PVA–metal-ion-coordinated ligand linker, which transfers ligand protons to the oxygen atoms of the PVA–metal-ion layer via the hydrogen-bonded network of the surrounding solvent molecules,[6] as shown in Figures S8–S10 in the Supporting Information. Figure S11 in the Supporting Information shows that ultrathin defect-free MOF membranes cannot form on the interface layer without the PVA–metal-ion layer, thus confirming the crucial role of this layer in the preparation of large-area ultrathin defect-free MOF membranes. The PVA–metal-ion layer dramatically promotes the deprotonation step, resulting in the facile formation of MOF nuclei. Upon MOF nucleation on the surface of this interfacial layer, the metal ions, ligand linkers, and building units consisting of desorbed metal ions coordinated with ligand
linkers in the solution continuously and topologically connect with the nuclei to form large-area ultrathin and defect-free MOF membranes.
As shown in Figures S8–S10 in the Supporting Information, three metal ions and two ligand linkers to construct the three exemplary types of MOFs were used to confirm the formation of the large-area ultrathin defect-free MOF membrane. The three MOFs show different Gibbs free energies, which increase in the order of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+} < \mathrm{Co}^{2+} < \mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\). Based on the results of control experiments, while the formation of the \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) (ZIF-67) membrane occurs rapidly, the formation of defects over the large area of this membrane cannot be completely avoided.[34] Moreover, CuBTC is unstable in water, so its synthesis necessitates the avoidance of pure water and instead the use of ethanol–water mixed solvent.[35] The stronger swelling characteristics of ethanol solvent compared with water restrain nucleus fixation within the polymer layer,[36] resulting in defects occurring in CuBTC membrane, as shown in Figure S12 in the Supporting Information. The use of ethanol–water mixed solvent enables the preparation of defect-free CuBTC membranes. After optimization, CuBTC membranes with dense and continuous morphology were successfully prepared using a ratio of ethanol:water of 3:1. Among the three MOFs investigated, ZIF-8 appears to be the most suitable for forming large-area ultrathin defect-free membranes.
