Figure 2 shows the morphologies and structures of the ZIF-8, ZIF-67, and CuBTC membranes. The compositions and preparation processes of the MOF nanoparticles and membranes are described in the Supporting Information. Visual inspection of the SEM images of the ZIF-8 membrane shows that it is continuous, defect free, and ultrathin (Figure 2a). ZIF-8 shows a uniform
surface and cross-sectional distribution over the entire \(2400\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) area, and the average thickness of the membrane is \({\approx}220\,\mathrm{nm}\) , as confirmed by subsequent analyses. A \(2400~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) membrane was divided into four parts (Figure S13a, Supporting Information), and each part displays similar and continuous SEM surfaces and cross-sectional morphologies (Figure 2a and Figures S13b–f and S14, Supporting Information).
Figure S15a,b in the Supporting Information shows the SEMenergy-dispersive X-ray (EDX) surface distributions of C, N, O, and Zn elements on the ZIF-8 membrane and a high-angle annular dark field-scanning transmission electron microscopy (ADFSTEM) cross-sectional image of the membrane prepared using a focused ion beam. Zn is uniformly distributed on the membrane, and the membrane is free of defects. Figure S15c in the Supporting Information shows the selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern of the ZIF-8 membrane. The sharp dots, which correspond to the (110) facet of ZIF-8, reflect its crystalline nature. The atomic force microscopy (AFM) image shown in Figure S15d in the Supporting Information indicates that the average surface roughness of the ZIF-8 membrane is \({\approx}87.35\ \mathrm{nm}\) .
The X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns in Figure 2a and Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) patterns in Figure S16a in the Supporting Information confirm the successful preparation of the ZIF-8 membranes. The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) results shown in Figure S16b,c in the Supporting Information demonstrate that the binding energy of the ZIF-8 membrane is lower than that of the ZIF-8 nanoparticles, which is mainly due to the large increase in open metal-ion sites in the mem
The X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns in Figure 2a and Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) patterns in Figure S16a in the Supporting Information confirm the successful preparation of the ZIF-8 membranes. The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) results shown in Figure S16b,c in the Supporting Information demonstrate that the binding energy of the ZIF-8 membrane is lower than that of the ZIF-8 nanoparticles, which is mainly due to the large increase in open metal-ion sites in the membrane. During ZIF-8 membrane formation, ligands near the interfacial layer tend to be protonated by the PVA coordinated with metal ions to form nuclei for ZIF-8 growth (Figure 1b and Figure S8, Supporting Information). Thus, the binding ability of these ligands with metal ions is lower than that of ZIF8 nanoparticles in solution, which facilitates the formation of open metal-ion sites. This supposition is supported by the slightly smaller pore diameter and Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of the membrane compared with those of the nanoparticles (Figure S16d,e, Supporting Information). In addition to a large surface area, the ZIF-8 membrane also shows high gas permeance and selectivity. Small samples were taken at different locations from a prepared large-area membrane (Figure S1, Supporting Information), and the gas separation performance of the ZIF-8 membrane was evaluated; the average value of all the test results was taken as the separation performance of the membrane. Figure S17 in the Supporting Information shows good pure- and mixed-gas separation performance of the ZIF8 membrane toward gas mixtures of \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) , \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}\) , and \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8}\) . This is due to the abundance of open metal-ion sites giving rise to the preferential adsorption of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) over \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}\) and \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}\) over \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8}\) . The open metal-ion sites on the pore walls bear a slight positive charge, thereby increasing their interactions with olefin gases,[37,38] and there is preferential adsorption of \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) over \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) , resulting from the \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}{-}\pi\) interactions between \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) and abundant organic ligand components.[39] In addition, the polarizability of \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) \((25.9\,\times\,10^{-25}\,\,\mathrm{cm}^{3})\) is greater than that of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) \((17.4~\times~10^{-25}~\,\mathrm{cm}^{3})\) , also resulting in selective preferential
adsorption of \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\).[40] The mixed-gas permeance of the membrane is slightly lower than its pure-gas permeance, which is mainly attributed to competitive adsorption between gas molecules. However, the mixed- and pure-gas selectivity of the membrane is nearly identical. This is also confirmed by comparing the gas adsorption isotherms of ZIF-8 membrane with conventional ZIF-8 nanoparticles, which indicates that solvent-free ZIF-8 nanoparticles exfoliated from the membrane surface have preferential adsorption selectivity (Figure S18, Supporting Information).
Ultrathin ZIF-67 and CuBTC membranes were also prepared using the same method employed to prepare the ZIF-8 membrane. The morphologies and structures of the ZIF-67 and CuBTC membranes are characterized in detail (Figure 2b,c and Figures S19–S26, Supporting Information), confirming that other MOFs could be successfully grown on the surface of the interfacial layer, although small defects were observed in membranes with an area of \(2400\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\). Figures S27 and S28 in the Supporting Information show the \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}\), and \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8}\) separation performance of the ZIF-67 and CuBTC
membranes. Compared with the ZIF-8 membrane, the Cu-BTC and ZIF-67 membranes display higher gas permeance but lower gas selectivity.
Figure 3a–c shows the successful preparation of a ZIF-8 membrane with an area of \(2400\;\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) and a ZIF-8 spiral-wound membrane module obtained from two pieces of the membrane with an area of \(4800\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\). Crimping to prepare the spiral-wound membrane module does not damage the structural integrity of the ZIF-8 membrane. Positions (1)–(4) shown in Figure 3b represent four positions of the ZIF-8 membrane module, and the red, green, yellow, and blue dotted lines shown in Figure 3c represent positions (1)–(4), respectively. As shown in Figure 3d,e, the surfaces of the ZIF-8 membrane at positions (1)–(4) appear to be free of defects, thus confirming that the ZIF-8 membrane has good crimping ability. The ZIF-8 membrane layer significantly improves the tensile strength of the membrane, and the ZIF-8 membrane exhibits reversible elastic behavior at a low elongation \((<\!4\%\), Figure 3f,g). As shown in Figure 3h,i, the ZIF-8 membrane demonstrates no obvious change in gas separation performance before crimping, after crimping, and after \(4\%\) elongation, which further demonstrates its integrity after crimping. When the elongation is greater than \(8\%\), the membrane surface becomes wrinkled, but no defects appear; this finding proves the
strong bond between the zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF) membrane and mPSf layers and good mechanical stability of the ZIF-8 membrane. Figure 3f-3 shows that when the elongation is greater than \(40\%\), the ZIF-8 membrane becomes obviously wrinkled, which is mainly attributed to the plastic deformation of the PDMS polymer in the mPSf layer (Figure 3f-4, elongation \(>\!40\%\)).
To further improve the separation performance of the membranes, we prepared a mixed metal-ion/ZIF membrane by using a \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\), and \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) mixed-ion composition. Figure 4a–c shows a prepared large-area mixed metal-ion/ZIF membrane with an area of \(2400\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) as well as the corresponding spiral-wound membrane module obtained from two pieces of the membrane with an area of \(4800\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\). The membrane was equally divided into four parts, and positions (1)–(4) were randomly selected from these four areas. As shown in Figure 4d–f and Figures S29 and S30 in the Supporting Information, based on the surface and cross-sectional images, the morphology of the mixed metal-ion/ZIF membrane at different positions appears to be uniform, ultrathin, and defect free. However, compared with those of the pure ZIF-8 membrane, the thickness and roughness of the mixed metal-ion/ZIF membrane (Figure 4f and Figure S31c, Supporting Information) slightly increased. The SEM-EDX
images in Figure S31a,b in the Supporting Information show that the metal elements are uniformly and densely distributed on the membrane. Figure S31d in the Supporting Information shows an ADF-STEM cross-sectional image of a mixed metal-ion/ZIF membrane prepared using a focused ion beam, along with a spatial map of the Zn element obtained from the EDX spectral image; here, the uniform distribution of the metal ions could clearly be observed.
Figure S32 in the Supporting Information shows the structural characterization of the mixed metal-ion/ZIF membrane. The XRD, FTIR, and SAED analyses (Figure S32a–c, Supporting Information) confirm it retains some characteristics of the ZIF-8 nanoparticles and has good crystallinity, which is mainly attributed to the fact that the reaction solution used to prepare the membrane contains \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) ions and 2-methylimidazole for synthesizing ZIF-8; the PVA layer contains only \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) ions that allow the step-nucleation in situ self-repair of defects. The XPS spectra shown in Figure S32d–f in the Supporting Information indicate that the binding energy of the mixed metal-ion/ZIF membrane is lower than that of the ZIF-8 nanoparticles, thus confirming the presence of a large number of unsaturated metal-ion sites in the membrane. The electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectra in Figure S32g in the Supporting Information show that with the doping of different metal ions, the EPR signal of the solvent-free stripped mixed metal-ion/ZIF nanoparticles is slightly stronger than that of the conventional ZIF-8 nanoparticles and solvent-free stripped ZIF-8 nanoparticles exfoliated from the pure ZIF-8 membrane. This finding demonstrates the presence of more nitrogen vacancies corresponding to open metal-ion sites in the mixed metal-ion/ZIF membrane, which also confirms an increase in unsaturated metal-ion sites. The BET surface area and adsorption–desorption isotherms shown in Figure S32h,i in the Supporting Information indicate that the specific surface area and gas adsorption performance of the mixed metal-ion/ZIF membrane are significantly lower than those of the ZIF-8 membrane. Figure S32j in the Supporting Information shows that the mixed metal-ion/ZIF membrane has a higher tensile strength compared with the mPSf, ZIF-67, and CuBTC membranes.
